B-6 decision summary:
- The registration half of B-3 is binding. Outside Development the host SHALL
  fail closed unless the BUILT SERVICE PROVIDER contains:
  - IDataProtectionProvider;
  - a non-null KeyManagementOptions.XmlRepository;
  - a non-null KeyManagementOptions.XmlEncryptor;
  - a repository whose implementation is not supplied by the ASP.NET Core Data
    Protection framework assembly;
  - an encryptor whose implementation is not supplied by that assembly.
- Intentionally rejected: EphemeralXmlRepository, FileSystemXmlRepository,
  ReadOnlyFileSystemXmlRepository, RegistryXmlRepository, NullXmlEncryptor,
  InvalidEncryptor, DpapiXmlEncryptor, DpapiNGXmlEncryptor,
  CertificateXmlEncryptor, and the framework's default local fallback. None is
  approved for this MVP.
- The intended production provider is Azure Blob Storage for the key repository
  and Azure Key Vault for key encryption.
- Configuration values alone do not satisfy the requirement. Documentation
  saying "do not deploy" is not a runtime safety control.
- Development may continue to use the explicitly approved local development
  behaviour.
- Recorded in prd.md (FR-42, AC-76, the Amendments table rows for
  HealthEndpointTests.cs and ShellPageTests.cs, and Open question E),
  architecture.md (A-2 and the startup-refusal section), and tasks.md (the
  binding-decisions section, the new T17A entry, and the T17 entry).
- Measured basis for treating a null repository as a failure: on ASP.NET Core 8
  a bare WebApplication registers no IDataProtectionProvider at all, and even
  after AddDataProtection() the XmlRepository stays null while the framework
  resolves a machine-local key directory plus DPAPI lazily.

